// Unlisted staff-only page: English translation of the staff Règlement
// Intérieur (see ../reglement-interieur/index.astro for the French
// original and the full rationale — same unlisted/noindex/no-sitemap
// treatment applies here). Translated by Claude from the French source;
// the French original remains the authoritative, legally binding
// version — the note banner below says so explicitly, and the download
// link at the bottom offers that original .docx (there is no separate
// English source document). To update: re-translate from the refreshed
// French page's docHtml and replace docHtml below, keeping the same
// paragraph-for-paragraph structure so the two stay easy to compare.
import '../../../styles/global.css';
import PageHero from '../../../components/PageHero.astro';

const pageUrl = 'https://brif.one/personnel/internal-regulations/';
const frenchUrl = '/personnel/reglement-interieur/';
const downloadHref = '/documents/personnel/reglement-interieur-personnel-aout-2026.docx';
const docHtml = `<p></p><p>BRITISH AND FRENCH ACADEMY</p><p><strong>INTERNAL REGULATIONS</strong></p><p><strong>Last updated: 10 August 2026</strong></p><p><strong>TEACHING STAFF</strong></p><p><em>Revision note: this version streamlines the Internal Regulations by removing purely internal execution details (who does what, with which tool, by what precise deadline), which are now set out in the SOP document (Standard Operating Procedures), available from Management. The Internal Regulations retain the full set of obligations and prohibitions applicable to every member of the community; only the purely internal operating details have been moved to the SOP, with each cross-reference flagged as (see SOP, system X).</em></p><p><strong>PREAMBLE</strong></p><p>British And French Academy (BRIF) is an educational institution founded on Christian values, while guaranteeing freedom of conscience and respect for everyone's rights. These Internal Regulations set out the rights and obligations of every member of the school community: staff, students, and parents.</p><p>Every member of staff, from the moment they take up their post at British And French Academy (BRIF), is required to read these Internal Regulations carefully, acknowledge receipt of them, and comply with them fully in the exercise of their duties. They undertake to respect their professional obligations, the school's procedures, Management's instructions, and the principles and values on which BRIF is founded.</p><p>These Regulations may be updated whenever changes in the organisation, the school's practices, or the applicable regulations make this necessary. Any change is brought to the attention of the members concerned and takes effect from the date of notification, subject to applicable legal provisions.</p><p><strong>PART II</strong></p><p><strong>STAFF REGULATIONS</strong></p><p><strong>1. GENERAL OBLIGATIONS AND PROFESSIONAL CONDUCT</strong></p><p><strong>1.1 Fundamental commitments</strong></p><p>Every member of staff undertakes to: follow the instructions of Management and its representatives; wear clothing that complies with the school's dress code; maintain professional conduct at all times, free of any inappropriate familiarity with students or colleagues; never use a personal phone during teaching hours; reserve the school's equipment and resources for strictly professional use; prepare lessons and teaching materials in advance, according to a schedule validated by Management via the Secretariat.</p><p><strong>1.2 Professional conduct towards students</strong></p><p>Every member of staff is expected to adopt an attitude of respect and professional care in their dealings with students. No student may be subjected to humiliation, degradation, intimidation, or any treatment incompatible with their dignity. This rule does not replace the requirements of pedagogical authority, but it sets the fundamental ethical framework for it.</p><p><strong>1.3 Professional humility and openness to change</strong></p><p>BRIF is an environment of continuous improvement. Every member of staff is expected to show professional humility, willingness to undergo training, and openness to the pedagogical direction set by Management. BRIF's staff commit to a process of continuous improvement and adopt contemporary standards of positive pedagogy.</p><p><strong>1.4 Initiatives and institutional communication</strong></p><p>Any member of staff wishing to initiate an action involving students, parents, or the school's image — in particular the production and distribution of photos, videos, or any other content — <strong>must inform Management in advance and obtain its explicit agreement</strong>. No initiative of this kind may be undertaken independently.</p><p><strong>1.5 Supervision and safety in class</strong></p><p>Teachers must <strong>never leave students unsupervised</strong> and must constantly monitor their appearance, behaviour, and language. Any teacher who observes inappropriate behaviour by a student in shared spaces, even outside their own class, is required to intervene or alert the relevant staff member: indifference towards students from other classes is not acceptable.</p><p><strong>2. PUNISHMENT, SANCTIONS, AND TREATMENT OF STUDENTS</strong></p><p><strong>2.1 Absolute prohibitions</strong></p><p>Every staff member is responsible for safeguarding the physical, psychological, and moral integrity of students. In accordance with the law and our Christian ethics, any form of verbal or physical violence is strictly prohibited.</p><p>Any repeated breach of these rules, despite warnings, will result in disciplinary sanctions that may go as far as dismissal, under the conditions provided for by applicable law and the employment contract.</p><p><strong>2.2 Reporting student misconduct</strong></p><p>Any disciplinary breach by a student, even a minor one, must be the subject of a <strong>written report</strong> to the person designated for this purpose by Management. No incident may be handled in isolation or concealed; complaints from parents regarding the treatment or behaviour of their children must be taken seriously and recorded in writing.</p><p>It is not for the teacher to handle a disciplinary problem, a repeat offence, or a difficulty with a student alone: this must be systematically and immediately reported to the Administration, which is responsible for informing parents and taking the appropriate measures. Any attempt to resolve a disciplinary incident independently, without informing the Administration, constitutes a professional failing.</p><p><strong>2.3 Ongoing awareness-raising</strong></p><p>Management may organise regular awareness sessions on students' rights, good disciplinary practice, and developments in educational standards, where appropriate with outside speakers. Attendance by all staff is <strong>mandatory</strong>.</p><p><strong>3. WORKING HOURS, PUNCTUALITY, AND ATTENDANCE</strong></p><p><strong>3.1 Working hours</strong></p><p>Teachers' working hours are set from <em>7:15 am to 4:30 pm</em>. Students actually enter class at <em>7:30 am</em>. Attendance and punctuality are essential professional obligations for the school to run properly.</p><p>Any teacher arriving late must report to the Secretariat and obtain an <em>entry pass</em> before joining their class; every instance of lateness is systematically recorded. Repeated lateness, whether unjustified or insufficiently justified, constitutes a professional failing that may give rise to the sanctions provided for in these Regulations, employment contracts, and applicable law; the periods not worked as a result may have corresponding consequences on pay, within the limits provided for by applicable law — this does not constitute a disciplinary fine and does not preclude any other applicable sanctions.</p><p>It is strictly forbidden to leave one's post or the school premises during working hours without Management's prior authorisation. Any authorised early departure must be reported to the Secretariat and duly recorded.</p><p><strong>3.2 Attendance and diligence</strong></p><ul><li>Every member of staff signs the attendance register on arrival and departure, including for any temporary absence during the day. Repeated failure to sign, even after being reminded, constitutes a disciplinary failing.</li><li>Any absence must be justified in writing to the Secretariat as soon as possible; an absence for medical reasons must be justified on return with a medical certificate, and a medical report will be required in the event of a prolonged absence.</li><li>Contract teachers make up the catch-up hours corresponding to hours missed.</li></ul><p><strong>3.3 Supervising students</strong></p><p>The teacher present before break time walks the students to the yard; the teacher taking the following lesson is responsible for collecting them on time. Homeroom teachers are responsible for <strong>their own students</strong> during outings, activities, and events, and may not delegate this responsibility without Management's explicit agreement. Students move in an orderly manner, supervised by their teachers; any disorderly movement is the responsibility of the teacher concerned.</p><p><strong>3.4 Attendance at events and activities</strong></p><p>Staff attendance at school activities and events (physical education sessions, <em>Assembly</em>, cultural activities, special events, club activities, and any other activity scheduled in the timetable or communicated by Management) is a full professional obligation, including for teachers who feel less comfortable athletically: supervising students remains required regardless of the teacher's own level.</p><p>During <strong>Open Days</strong>, attendance by all teachers is mandatory, with each one receiving the parents of their own students. Management may call emergency meetings outside normal hours; attendance at these is mandatory except in the case of a duly justified major impediment.</p><p><em>(Any refreshments Management may offer during these events are a matter of courtesy and do not, under any circumstances, constitute a contractual obligation.)</em></p><p><strong>4. PEDAGOGICAL ORGANISATION</strong></p><p><strong>4.1 Curriculum and planning</strong></p><p>An annual lesson plan is mandatory before the start of the school year and must be submitted to Management via the Secretariat; each teacher also produces a weekly and termly programme. The designated Coordinator monitors this on an ongoing basis. The absence of a structured programme, or failure to follow the approved programme, constitutes a punishable pedagogical failing.</p><p>The digital platform designated by Management (currently Google Classroom) is BRIF's official tool for tracking teaching; its use is mandatory for submitting programmes, progressions, assessments, and any teaching document requested by Management <em>(precise arrangements: see SOP, 2.14)</em>.</p><p><strong>4.2 Classroom teaching practice</strong></p><p>Teachers provide active, structured, high-quality teaching: the teacher stands and uses the board; lessons have real, structured content suited to the students' level; teaching of every subject on the curriculum, including singing and expressive/motor-skills activities, is delivered rigorously and regularly. Teachers implement, on a daily basis: clear learning objectives, regular review of what has been learned, step-by-step explanations, checks for understanding, live feedback to students, and work adapted to the different levels within the class.</p><p>A committee of teachers, formed for certain classes, is responsible for regularly refreshing the repertoire of songs taught as part of Assemblies; attendance by the designated teachers is mandatory <em>(composition and operation: see SOP, 2.14)</em>.</p><p><strong>4.3 Quality of teaching materials</strong></p><p>Course materials, tests, and homework must be professional, aligned with the curriculum, typed with consistent formatting, and submitted to the Secretariat in a clean, print-ready format. The Secretariat is not responsible for correcting or reformatting teaching documents: any submission that does not meet these standards is returned to the teacher for correction. The use of artificial intelligence in preparing tests is permitted but does not relieve the teacher of sole responsibility for the content, curriculum compliance, and formal quality of the document produced.</p><p><em>(Precise formatting standards, and the handling of illustrations and symbols: see SOP, 2.14.)</em></p><p><strong>4.4 Deadlines for submitting documents</strong></p><p>The minimum deadline for submitting tests to the Secretariat is set at <strong>three weeks</strong> before the planned date of use, to allow time for proofreading, approval by Management, and printing. Documents to be photocopied other than tests are submitted at least 3 days in advance. Any teacher who is regularly late with these deadlines is responsible for this and risks a formal sanction.</p><p><strong>4.5 Assessments and homework</strong></p><p>Every piece of homework or assessment reinforces what has been taught without introducing material that has not been covered, in a reasonable amount suited to the students' age. Exam papers and marking schemes are approved in advance by Management; the pedagogical coordinator for each section (French or English) checks the corresponding assessments. Marked papers are submitted to the Secretariat, which forwards them to Management within the required timeframe.</p><p><strong>4.6 Textbooks</strong></p><p>Any textbook prescribed to families must be used in class, regularly and in line with the curriculum: it is unacceptable for a textbook purchased by families to go unused all year. Any teacher who does not meet this obligation must account for it to Management.</p><p><strong>4.7 Monitoring students and marking exercise books</strong></p><p>Students' exercise books are marked regularly, at least once a week, with the teacher's mark on every single exercise book in their class — not just some of them. A weekly check on this is organised by the school <em>(arrangements: see SOP, 2.14)</em>.</p><p>Every student's marks and results must be checked before being issued or communicated; shortcomings must be noted and reported, never concealed. Concerning results are communicated to parents as soon as possible after marking, not at the end of term. Every student absence is recorded together with the work and homework missed, and teachers put together a support plan for students who are struggling.</p><p><strong>4.8 Individual support and fairness</strong></p><p>Any individual support (tutorials, catch-up support, supplementary activities) must produce observable, measurable results. Any favouritism in how students are supported or assessed is <strong>strictly forbidden</strong>.</p><p><strong>4.9 Report card comments</strong></p><p>Comments on report cards must be personalised, precise, and a true reflection of each student's profile. Generic remarks, or ones copied from one student to another, are unacceptable.</p><p><strong>4.10 Periodic teacher reports</strong></p><p>Every teacher produces, as far as possible, a weekly report on the progress of the programme and any difficulties encountered, as well as a <strong>mandatory monthly report</strong> summarising the work carried out and the state of student progress. Any notable event warranting an immediate ad hoc report (persistent lack of work, a serious disciplinary incident, worrying behaviour) is passed on to Management without delay via the Secretariat, independently of these periodic reports <em>(recipient and precise timing: see SOP, 5.10)</em>.</p><p><strong>5. ROLES AND RESPONSIBILITIES WITHIN THE TEAM</strong></p><p><strong>5.1 Internal organisation</strong></p><p>The school has staff with complementary roles: a coordinator responsible for monitoring exercise books; a coordinator responsible for monitoring lessons, authorised to carry out unannounced classroom visits; a coordinator for student life and general supervision, the main point of contact for any disciplinary matter; a coordinator for each pedagogical section (French Nursery, English Nursery, French Primary, English Primary), under the overall coordination of the Head of Pedagogy appointed by Management. Every teacher works in liaison with the coordinator for their section and follows the pedagogical direction agreed with Management. Every member of staff cooperates with these coordinators and may not obstruct them in carrying out their duties.</p><p><strong>5.2 Team spirit and collective responsibility</strong></p><p>Every member of staff shares responsibility for the smooth running of the school and the wellbeing of <strong>all</strong> students, not only those in their own class. A teacher who notices that a student is alone, in difficulty, or in an inappropriate situation in a shared space is required to intervene or alert the relevant staff member, even outside their own class. Professional isolation or indifference towards students from other classes is not acceptable.</p><p><strong>5.3 Unacceptable professional conduct</strong></p><p>Staff maintain constant vigilance and an active stance throughout lessons and supervision duties.</p><p><strong>6. PROFESSIONAL RELATIONS</strong></p><p><strong>6.1 With Management</strong> — Teachers work with the pedagogical leadership and subject coordinators, accept unannounced pedagogical visits, take part in mandatory training and meetings (including outside normal hours), and respect the confidentiality of pedagogical decisions taken collectively.</p><p><strong>6.2 With colleagues</strong> — Teamwork is a professional requirement: mutual support, communication, and solidarity are expected of everyone, with mutual respect and dignity.</p><p><strong>6.3 With parents</strong> — No teacher is authorised to arrange a meeting with parents without Management's agreement, or to communicate school information directly without authorisation. It is formally forbidden to approach parents about settling sums owed to the school, or any financial contribution in any form whatsoever, including during school events.</p><p><strong>7. SCHOOL CELEBRATIONS, CEREMONIES, AND EVENTS</strong></p><p><strong>7.1 Organisation and punctuality</strong></p><p>School celebrations and ceremonies showcase the school: they must be organised rigorously and professionally. Every teacher involved is present at the appointed time, ready to play their part. Students are given adequate breaks and mealtimes during long events.</p><p><strong>7.2 Rehearsals and preparing students</strong></p><p>Rehearsals are conducted with strict respect for students' status; under no circumstances may they be treated as hired performers or as acting under duress. If any proceeds, gifts, or rewards are collected at these events, they are shared fairly and transparently with the students involved, after discussion with Management.</p><p><strong>7.3 Prohibitions during events</strong></p><p>At any celebration or ceremony, it is <strong>strictly forbidden</strong> to: approach a parent for money while their child is on stage, in a parade, or performing; keep a student on stage while waiting for payment from parents; discuss the distribution of funds collected in front of students or parents; leave students unsupervised or without a meal during long events. Any breach of these rules will be treated as a serious professional offence.</p><p><strong>8. CONDUCT FORBIDDEN TO STAFF</strong></p><p><strong>8.1</strong> Staff are strictly forbidden to: enter the school premises under the influence of alcohol or drugs; smoke or vape on the school premises; use school equipment for personal purposes; sell to or buy anything from students or their parents; bring animals, outside persons, or equipment onto the premises without Management's prior authorisation; post or distribute any document not approved by Management; publish defamatory content about the school; collect funds without Management's express authorisation; bring into class any material or content outside the official curriculum without prior approval; take any initiative involving students or the school's image without informing Management beforehand.</p><p><strong>8.2 Discretion, professional confidentiality, and loyalty to the school</strong></p><p>Members of staff are bound by a strict duty of discretion, as well as an obligation of loyalty and professional confidentiality towards the school community, and towards parents in particular.</p><p>On this basis, it is formally forbidden to discuss with members of the school community (parents, legal guardians, or third parties) private disputes, financial disagreements, or questions relating to pay. Any claim or question relating to working conditions or pay is a matter for internal channels alone and must be addressed directly to the school's Management.</p><p>Any breach of this rule that is liable to harm the school's reputation, credibility, or financial stability will constitute a disciplinary offence liable to the sanctions provided for in these Regulations.</p><p><strong>9. HARASSMENT AND ABUSE OF AUTHORITY</strong></p><p>Any form of moral or sexual harassment is <strong>formally forbidden</strong>, in accordance with the law. No staff member may be sanctioned for reporting an actual or suspected case of harassment. Any proven breach will result in disciplinary proceedings that may go as far as <strong>dismissal</strong>.</p><p><strong>10. STAFF DISCIPLINARY PROCEDURES</strong></p><p><strong>10.1 General sanctions</strong> — (1) Written warning; (2) Reprimand; (3) Suspension; (4) Dismissal.</p><p><strong>10.2 Staff on probation or on temporary contracts</strong> — (1) Written warning; (2) Reprimand; (3) Temporary exclusion (maximum two months); (4) Termination of contract.</p><p><em>(The investigation and decision process applicable to these two scales: see SOP, 6.1.)</em></p><p><strong>11. APPEARANCE, HYGIENE, AND CONDUCT OF TEACHERS IN CLASS</strong></p><p>The following articles set out, in concrete terms, the requirements for appearance, hygiene, and conduct expected of every teacher in the day-to-day exercise of their duties. They supplement the general obligations in Section 1 and the prohibitions set out in Sections 2 and 8.</p><p><strong>11.1 Personal hygiene</strong></p><p>Impeccable personal and clothing hygiene is required, in keeping with public health standards and the school's image.</p><p><strong>11.2 Dress</strong></p><p>Dress must be decent, clean, and professional, in line with the school's dress code. Any adjustment of clothing is done away from students. Backless shoes (such as babouches, slides, or mule-style sandals) are forbidden, as are transparent, tight-fitting, low-cut, or torn clothing, and sportswear outside physical education lessons.</p><p><strong>11.3 Conduct and use of devices in class</strong></p><p>Wearing earphones is forbidden on school premises during working hours. Using a phone for personal purposes is forbidden during lessons and supervision duty (see 1.1). Eating or drinking in front of students in class is forbidden, with the exception of water (see 5.3). A teacher's humour must never target a student: mocking or commenting on a student's appearance, background, family, or results in front of the class constitutes a serious offence (see 2.1).</p><p><strong>11.4 Daily administrative and pedagogical record-keeping</strong></p><p>The teacher starts their lesson at the exact time it is due to begin. The class logbook (paper or electronic) is filled in at the end of each lesson: it is an official document, subject to checks by Management and available for parents to consult. The class communication notebook is kept up to date. Unannounced classroom inspections, checking of monthly reports, and coordinating the Teacher on Duty rota are the responsibility of the Head of Student Supervision <em>(detailed arrangements: see SOP, 5.10)</em>.</p><p><strong>11.5 Integrity, propriety, and confidentiality</strong></p><p>Consuming alcohol or tobacco is forbidden on the school premises, in its immediate surroundings, and in any situation where the teacher is identifiable as a member of staff (see 8); any breach is reported to Management and, where applicable, to the Ministry of Education. Giving paid private lessons to one's own students is forbidden without Management's prior written authorisation. Teachers are bound by confidentiality regarding results, family situations, and personal information concerning students.</p><p><strong>DECLARATION OF COMMITMENT</strong></p><p><strong>For Staff:</strong></p><p>I, the undersigned, declare that I have read the Internal Regulations for Staff of British And French Academy and undertake to comply with them fully in the exercise of my duties.</p><p>Full name: _______________________ Position: _______________________ Date: _______________________ Signature: _______________________</p>`;
---
